For Young’s double-slit fringes, the angular separation between adjacent bright fringes is approximately

\[
\Delta \theta \approx \frac{\lambda}{d}
\]

The fringes just disappear when this angular separation equals the eye’s angular resolution:

\[
\frac{\lambda}{d} = 1' = \frac{1}{60}^\circ \approx 2.91\times 10^{-4}\,\text{rad}
\]

For sodium light, take

\[
\lambda \approx 589\,\text{nm} = 5.89\times 10^{-7}\,\text{m}
\]

So,

\[
d = \frac{\lambda}{\Delta \theta}
= \frac{5.89\times 10^{-7}}{2.91\times 10^{-4}}
\approx 2.0\times 10^{-3}\,\text{m}
\]

Answer: \(\boxed{2.0\times 10^{-3}\ \text{m} \approx 2\ \text{mm}}\)

